---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../styles/articles.css';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Layout title="Standards & Terminology — CABE" description="The IETF foundations of CABE and the role of related CCEB and NATO publications, interoperability mappings and alternative technologies.">
  <main class="page" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <header class="page__header">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="section-label">Reference</span>
        <h1 class="page__title">Standards &amp; Terminology</h1>
        <p class="page__subtitle">The publications behind CABE and how they relate to one another.</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="page__body">
      <div class="container article-prose">
        <section aria-labelledby="relationship-title">
          <h2 id="relationship-title">How the references fit together</h2>
          <p>CABE is an independent family of specifications. Its core architecture does not require NATO or CCEB infrastructure. Additional specifications, including <a href={`${base}/spec/acp240/`}>CABE-ACP240</a>, define mappings for particular interoperability environments. Such mappings do not by themselves establish conformance with every requirement of the referenced publications.</p>
          <p>A <strong>normative dependency</strong> supplies requirements used by a specification. An <strong>integration mapping</strong> translates selected concepts for interoperability. A <strong>related standard</strong> provides context for a deployment, while an <strong>alternative technology</strong> offers another approach. This page is informative; the <a href={`${base}/specification/`}>CABE specifications</a> define the requirements and normative references.</p>

          <div class="reference-table" role="region" aria-label="Standards and their relationship to CABE" tabindex="0">
            <table>
              <caption>Publications, provenance and relationship to CABE</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Reference</th><th scope="col">Publisher / provenance</th><th scope="col">Relationship to CABE</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc8949">CBOR</a> / <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9052">COSE</a></th>
                  <td>Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF)</td>
                  <td><strong>Normative dependencies.</strong> CBOR supplies the encoding and COSE the cryptographic structures used by the <a href={`${base}/spec/cbes/`}>CABE Baseline Envelope Structure (CBES)</a>. See RFC 8949, RFC 9052 and <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9053">RFC 9053</a>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">ACP 240(A)</th>
                  <td>Combined Communications-Electronics Board (CCEB)</td>
                  <td><strong>Integration mapping.</strong> ACP 240 provides a framework for data-centric interoperability. <a href={`${base}/spec/acp240/`}>CABE-ACP240</a> maps selected labelling concepts relevant to that framework; it does not implement the whole publication.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">STANAG 4774 / <a href="https://nso.nato.int/nso/nsdd/main/standards/ap-details/1967/EN">ADatP-4774</a></th>
                  <td>NATO / NATO Standardization Office (NSO)</td>
                  <td><strong>Integration mapping.</strong> Confidentiality metadata label syntax and semantics. ADatP-4774 is a normative reference of <a href={`${base}/spec/acp240/`}>CABE-ACP240</a>, which maps selected label fields to CABE Attributes.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">STANAG 4778 / ADatP-4778</th>
                  <td>NATO / NSO</td>
                  <td><strong>Related standard.</strong> Metadata-to-data binding. Its binding mechanism is not automatically interchangeable with the cryptographic construction defined by <a href={`${base}/spec/cbes/`}>CBES</a>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">STANAG 5663 / ADatP-5663</th>
                  <td>NATO / NSO</td>
                  <td><strong>Related standard.</strong> Federated identity, credential and access management context. It is not a dependency of the core <a href={`${base}/spec/arch/`}>CABE architecture</a> or <a href={`${base}/spec/ckap/`}>Key Access Protocol</a>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">ZTDF / <a href="https://opentdf.io/">OpenTDF</a></th>
                  <td>Related format and open-source project</td>
                  <td><strong>Alternative technologies.</strong> Zero Trust Data Format (ZTDF) and the OpenTDF project are useful points of comparison. Neither is a CABE conformance requirement; the format and the implementation project are distinct.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="publication-title">
          <h2 id="publication-title">Publication names and sources</h2>
          <p><strong>ACP</strong> means Allied Communications Publication. ACP 240 is a CCEB publication. <strong>STANAG</strong> means NATO Standardization Agreement; the associated Allied Data Processing Publication (<strong>ADatP</strong>) contains the technical specification. These identifiers describe different publications and responsibilities.</p>
          <p>Use the <a href="https://wss.apan.org/s/CCEB/public/Forms/acps.aspx">CCEB publications catalogue</a> for ACP publications and the <a href="https://nso.nato.int/nso/nsdd/main/standards">NATO Standardization Document Database</a> for STANAGs and associated publications, including ADatP-4774, ADatP-4778 and ADatP-5663. Publication access may require the relevant national or organisational channel. For the editions cited by the mapping, see the <a href={`${base}/spec/acp240/#normative-references`}>CABE-ACP240 normative references</a>.</p>
          <p>The <code>NATO</code> policy identifier and NATO classification levels used in CABE-ACP240 retain their specified meaning. Correctly attributing ACP 240 to CCEB does not change those values or the mapping requirements.</p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .reference-table { margin-top: 24px; overflow-x: auto; }
  table { width: 100%; min-width: 700px; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.75; }
  caption { text-align: left; color: var(--quiet); font-size: 13px; margin-bottom: 12px; }
  th, td { padding: 18px 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); text-align: left; vertical-align: top; }
  thead th { background: var(--soft); color: var(--ink); font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  tbody th { width: 22%; color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  td:nth-child(2) { width: 23%; }
  @media print {
    .reference-table { overflow: visible; }
    table { min-width: 0; font-size: 10pt; }
    th, td { padding: 10px; }
    tr { break-inside: avoid; }
  }
</style>
